Here we have used that a Pasch configuration has 24 automorphisms (we can embed a Pasch configuration as a subgraph of $K^{(3)}_{2,2,2}$, in such a way that half of the $3!\cdot2^3=48$ automorphisms of $K^{(3)}_{2,2,2}$ are also automorphisms of the Pasch configuration). We have also used that the contribution of ``degenerate'' Pasch configurations (in which two of $x_0,x_1,y_0,y_1,z_0,z_1$ coincide) is at most $O(n^{5})\cdot O(n^{-4})=o(m)$. In addition, we have used the elementary estimate $\log(1-q)=-q+O(q^2)$.

